*(
    title: Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation
    description: Two worked cases of homeostasis — holding temperature steady, and holding water and salt balance steady.
    tags: thermoregulation, osmoregulation, kidney, endotherm, module 8, biology
    lesson
)

// Scene 3's figures: a healthy adult filters about 125 mL of plasma per minute
// at the glomeruli, which is 125 x 60 x 24 = 180 000 mL, or 180 L, per day, and
// excretes about 1.5 L of urine. So 178.5 L is reabsorbed — 99.2 per cent of
// what was filtered.
@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    The general shape of a homeostatic loop is easier to trust once it has been followed through a real one, and temperature is the case where the two strategies available to an animal are furthest apart.

    The canvas plots body temperature against the temperature of the surroundings. A mammal's line is flat: at five degrees outside and at thirty-five, its core stays near $37\,^\circ\text{C}$. A lizard's line has a slope of one — its body is whatever the environment is.

    definition{
        An animal that maintains its body temperature within narrow limits using heat generated by its own metabolism. Mammals and birds are the endothermic groups.
    }(
        term: Endotherm
    )

    definition{
        An animal whose body temperature is determined mainly by its surroundings, and which regulates it, when it does, by moving between warmer and cooler places rather than by generating heat.
    }(
        term: Ectotherm
    )

    ===

    Calling the flat line "better" misses what it costs. Holding a core temperature above the surroundings means generating heat continuously, and a mammal's resting metabolic rate is several times that of a reptile of the same mass — which is to say it must find and eat several times as much food, every day, whether or not it does anything.

    What the flat line buys is independence. An endotherm can hunt at night, stay active through winter, and live at latitudes an ectotherm cannot, because its enzymes are always at their optimum. An ectotherm buys cheapness instead: a large lizard can survive on a fraction of the food, and can simply wait out conditions a mammal would have to eat its way through.

    note{
        The flat line has ends. Below and above the range shown, an endotherm's regulation fails too — heat production cannot keep up in extreme cold, and heat loss cannot keep up in extreme heat. Hypothermia and heat stroke are both the same mechanism being overwhelmed rather than a different mechanism operating.
    }
}[
    canvas.graph{
        curve(f: 37; domain: [0, 45]; colour: blue; label: endotherm)

        cue{
            curve(f: x; domain: [0, 45]; colour: orange; label: ectotherm)
        }(
            in: 1
        )
    }(
        x axis label: temperature of the surroundings ($^\circ$C)
        y axis label: body temperature ($^\circ$C)
        x domain: [0, 45]
        y domain: [0, 45]
    )
]

scene{
    # Three ways to move heat, and both strategies use all of them

    Whatever an animal's thermal strategy, the heat has to actually go somewhere, and there are only three kinds of thing an organism can do about it. The table sorts the responses that way rather than by species, because the same three categories apply to a kangaroo and to a bearded dragon.

    definition{
        A response that changes an organism's rate of heat gain or loss without changing its structure or its position: sweating, panting, shivering, and altering blood flow to the skin.
    }(
        term: Physiological response
    )

    Structural features are the slowest to change and cannot be switched off, which is why they suit a predictable climate. Behavioural responses are free and immediate and are what an ectotherm relies on almost entirely. Physiological responses are the ones under moment-to-moment homeostatic control, and are what the receptor–control centre–effector loop is actually operating.

    The hypothalamus is the control centre for all of it. It carries thermoreceptors monitoring the temperature of the blood passing through it, receives reports from receptors in the skin, and compares both against a set point near $37\,^\circ\text{C}$.

    Too hot, and it triggers vasodilation — arterioles supplying the skin widen, more warm blood flows near the surface, and more heat is lost — together with sweating, whose cooling comes from the evaporation rather than from the water itself. Too cold, and it triggers vasoconstriction, shivering, and in many mammals the raising of hair to trap an insulating layer of air.

    Both sets of responses are negative feedback, and both change the very temperature the hypothalamus is measuring.
}[
    table{
        Kind of response | An endotherm                                                              | An ectotherm
        Structural       | fur, blubber, a large body with a small surface in cold climates; large ears in hot ones | scales limiting water loss; body colour affecting how fast it absorbs radiation
        Behavioural      | seeking shade, huddling, a koala pressing itself against a cool tree trunk  | basking on a warm rock in the morning, retreating underground at midday
        Physiological    | sweating, panting, shivering, vasodilation and vasoconstriction, a kangaroo licking its forearms | very limited — some lizards alter blood flow to the skin while basking, but heat is not generated internally
    }(
        title: The same three categories, used differently
        highlight rows: 4
        highlight colour: orange
    )
]

scene{
    # The kidney throws almost everything away and takes it straight back

    Water balance is regulated by an organ whose method looks, at first, absurdly wasteful. The kidneys filter about $180$ litres of fluid out of the blood every day — around forty times the entire blood volume — and then reabsorb nearly all of it, excreting about $1.5$ litres as urine.

    definition{
        The regulation of the water and salt concentration of the body's fluids, so that cells are neither swollen by dilute surroundings nor shrunken by concentrated ones.
    }(
        term: Osmoregulation
    )

    ===

    Filtration at the glomerulus is indiscriminate: blood pressure forces water and everything small enough dissolved in it — glucose, amino acids, ions, urea — through into the tubule, holding back only cells and large proteins. Nothing is selected at this stage, which is why so much has to be recovered afterwards.

    ===

    Reabsorption is where the selection happens, and it happens by taking back what is wanted rather than by having excluded what is not. The proximal tubule reabsorbs all the glucose, all the amino acids and most of the water and salt; the loop of Henle establishes a concentration gradient in the tissue around it; the distal tubule and collecting duct then fine-tune what is finally released.

    The advantage of doing it this way is that the fine-tuning is the last step, so a single adjustment at the end changes the composition of the urine without anything upstream having to change. Filter everything, take back what you need, and adjust how much you take back — which is exactly what a variable-output regulator requires.

    ===

    Two hormones do the adjusting. Antidiuretic hormone, released by the pituitary when the hypothalamus detects that the blood is too concentrated, makes the collecting duct permeable to water, so more water is reabsorbed and a small volume of concentrated urine is produced. Aldosterone, from the adrenal cortex, increases sodium reabsorption in the distal tubule, and water follows the sodium.

    Both are negative feedback. Reabsorbing water dilutes the blood, which is what the hypothalamus was measuring, so ADH release falls — and the loop is the same one the previous internote set out, with a hormone as the signal and a stretch of tubule as the effector.
}[
    canvas.diagram{
        box#filter{
            Glomerulus — filtration
            about $180$ L a day forced out of the blood, indiscriminately
        }

        cue.draw{
            box#proximal{
                Proximal tubule — reabsorption
                all the glucose and amino acids, most of the water and salt
            }
            arrow(from: #filter; to: #proximal)
        }(
            in: 1
        )

        cue.draw{
            box#loop{
                Loop of Henle
                builds a salt gradient in the surrounding tissue
            }
            box#distal{
                Distal tubule and collecting duct
                the adjustable step, where the final amount is decided
            }
            arrow(from: #proximal; to: #loop)
            arrow(from: #loop; to: #distal)
        }(
            in: 2
        )

        cue.draw{
            box#urine:terminal{
                About $1.5$ L of urine
                $99$ per cent of what was filtered has been taken back
            }
            arrow(from: #distal; to: #urine)
        }(
            in: 3
        )
    }(
        flow: down
        title: What happens to the filtrate
    )
]

scene{
    ## The plant's version of the same problem

    A plant faces both problems at once and can walk away from neither. It cannot move into the shade, and it loses water through the same pores it must open to obtain carbon dioxide, so every response it has is a compromise rather than a solution.

    Water loss is the more serious of the two, and the table lists the features Australian plants in particular have arrived at. Every one of them works by the same principle: reduce the water vapour gradient at the leaf surface, or reduce the surface itself.

    Temperature is handled largely as a by-product. Transpiration cools a leaf in the same way sweating cools skin, which means that a plant with plenty of water keeps its leaves below air temperature — and a plant short of water, having closed its stomata, loses that cooling exactly when it most needs it. The two regulated variables are coupled through one opening, and a plant cannot optimise both.

    task{
        Spinifex leaves roll into a tight cylinder in dry conditions, with the stomata on the inner surface. Explain how this reduces water loss, and say what it costs the plant.
    }[
        Rolling encloses the stomata in a still, humid chamber. Water vapour leaving them accumulates there instead of being carried away, so the gradient between the inside of the leaf and the air immediately outside it becomes shallow and transpiration slows itself. The cost is carbon dioxide: the same enclosed air is also depleted of it, and the leaf's photosynthesis is limited for as long as it stays rolled — which is why the leaves unroll when water is available again.
    ](
        title: Your turn
    )
}[
    table{
        Feature                              | How it reduces water loss
        Sunken stomata, in pits              | vapour accumulates in the pit, so the gradient at the pore is shallower
        Hairs on the leaf surface            | they trap a still, humid layer against the leaf, doing the same thing over the whole surface
        Rolled leaves, as in spinifex        | the stomata are enclosed in a humid chamber inside the roll
        Reduced leaves, as in mulga and other acacias | less surface, and what remains is held vertically, so it intercepts less midday sun
        A thick waxy cuticle                 | water cannot cross the epidermis anywhere except at a stoma
        Stomata opening at night, as in succulents | carbon dioxide is taken in and stored when the air is cool and humid, and the pores stay shut through the day
    }(
        title: How a plant that cannot move manages water
    )
]
